<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">ʻIolani Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Iolani Palace</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">ʻIolani Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> was the royal residence of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, completed in 1882 for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a> and lit by electricity from 1887. After the overthrow of the monarchy in 1893 it served as the seat of the provisional government, the Republic of Hawaii and later the territorial government. Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a> was imprisoned in one of its rooms in 1895 after a failed royalist rising.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iolani-palace.jpg" alt="ʻIolani Palace" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gage Skidmore from Surprise, AZ, United States of America · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iolani_Palace_(51872681413).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In July 1844 the chief Kekūanaōʻa began building a large home on the site as a gift to his daughter Victoria Kamāmalu. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> bought the estate and used the house, called Hale Aliʻi, meaning House of the Chiefs, as his royal residence after he moved his capital from Lāhainā to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> in 1845. It was built as a traditional aliʻi residence with ceremonial spaces only, a throne room, a reception room and a state dining room, and the monarch slept elsewhere. Under Kamehameha V the Privy Council preferred the name ʻIolani Palace, from one of the late Kamehameha IV's given names, ʻIolani, after the royal hawk.</p><p>By the time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">David Kalākaua</a> took the throne the old building was in poor condition, damaged by termites, and he ordered it razed. Having travelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, he commissioned a new palace inspired by its grand palaces, across the street from Aliʻiōlani Hale.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Thomas J. Baker designed the structure, with Charles J. Wall and Isaac Moore contributing details. The cornerstone was laid on 31 December 1879 and the building was completed in November 1882 at a cost of over $340,000. Of brick with concrete facing, it measures about 140 feet by 100 feet and rises two storeys over a raised basement, with four corner towers and two in the centre. Its style is known as American Florentine. A staircase of koa wood rises from the grand hall, and the throne room, the blue room and the dining room adjoin it. A formal coronation ceremony for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a> was held on 12 February 1883, although he had reigned for nine years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>After the monarchy was overthrown by the Committee of Safety in 1893, troops of the Provisional Government took the palace, and it later served the Republic of Hawaiʻi. Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a> was imprisoned for nine months in a small upper room after the second of the Wilcox rebellions in 1895, and her trial was held in the former throne room. On 12 August 1898 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> troops raised the American flag at the palace to mark annexation. It then housed the Territory of Hawaiʻi, served as military headquarters in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and was the first state capitol, until government offices moved out in 1969. Liliʻuokalani received a state funeral in the throne room in 1917.</p><p>The palace was restored by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Friends</a> of ʻIolani Palace and opened to the public as a museum in 1978. It was designated a National Historic Landmark on 29 December 1962, and it is the only royal palace on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> soil. In 2008 a group of native Hawaiians calling themselves the Hawaiian Kingdom Government occupied it in protest at American rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “ʻIolani Palace” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>ʻIolani Palace</b> in Honolulu was lit by electricity by 1887, four years before electric lighting was installed in the White House in Washington in 1891.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>She translated it into English while held under house arrest in <b>ʻIolani Palace</b>; the translation was published in 1897 and republished by Pueo Press in 1978.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kumulipo">Kumulipo</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;The Queen's Prayer&quot;, in Hawaiian &quot;Ke Aloha O Ka Haku&quot;, is a mele she composed on 22 March 1895 while she was held under house arrest at <b>ʻIolani Palace</b>, and dedicated to her niece and heir apparent, Victoria Kaʻiulani.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-queens-prayer">The Queen's Prayer</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1895 royalists led by Robert Wilcox attempted a counter-revolution, which failed; Liliʻuokalani was arrested, forced to abdicate and imprisoned in <b>ʻIolani Palace</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-hawaii">Republic of Hawaii</a></li></ul></section>
